INSERT INTO `women_awareness_articles` VALUES (1,'Understanding Domestic Violence Law in India','Legal Rights','Understand the Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005, the forms of domestic violence it covers, and the support available to an affected woman.','The Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act (PWDVA), 2005 protects women from domestic violence within a domestic relationship. It recognizes different forms of abuse, including physical, sexual, verbal or emotional, and economic abuse.\n\nKey protections include:\n\n1. Protection Orders: A Magistrate may issue orders to prevent further acts of domestic violence.\n\n2. Residence Rights: The Act recognizes a woman\'s right to reside in a shared household, subject to the provisions of the Act.\n\n3. Monetary Relief: A Magistrate may grant monetary relief for expenses and losses resulting from domestic violence.\n\n4. Custody Orders: The Magistrate may make temporary custody arrangements concerning children.\n\n5. Compensation: Compensation may be granted for injuries, including mental torture and emotional distress.\n\nHow to seek help:\n\n- Contact the Protection Officer or an authorized service provider.\n- Approach the police, One Stop Centre or other support service.\n- An application can be made to the Magistrate under Section 12 of the Act.\n- A Domestic Incident Report (DIR) may be prepared with assistance from the Protection Officer or service provider.\n\nA woman can seek help without first having to prepare every piece of evidence herself. Keep available documents, messages, medical records and other relevant material where it is safe to do so.\n\nEmergency support is also available through 112, and Women Helpline 181 can connect women with support services.','2026-09-03','Legal Aid Team','2026-09-03 12:06:16'),(2,'How to File an FIR: A Step-by-Step Guide','Legal Rights','Understand how information about a cognizable offence can be given to the police and what to do if you face difficulty.','An FIR is the initial police record of information relating to a cognizable offence.\n\nUnder the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita (BNSS), 2023, information relating to a cognizable offence is dealt with under Section 173.\n\nWhat you can do:\n\n1. Give the information to the police station dealing with the matter.\n2. Clearly describe what happened, when and where it happened, and provide relevant details.\n3. Preserve available evidence such as messages, photographs, documents or other records.\n4. Ask for the details or copy of the recorded information as applicable.\n5. Note the police station, date and any reference or FIR number provided to you.\n\nIf there is difficulty in getting the information recorded, BNSS provides further mechanisms for approaching senior police authorities and the Magistrate, depending on the circumstances.\n\nFor emergencies, call 112.\n\nFor crimes involving women, special procedures may apply depending on the offence and circumstances. Seek assistance from the police, a lawyer, a legal services authority or a women\'s support service when needed.','2026-09-03','Legal Aid Team','2026-09-03 12:06:16'),(3,'Workplace Sexual Harassment: Your Rights Under POSH','Workplace Rights','Learn how the POSH Act protects women from sexual harassment at the workplace and how a complaint can be made.','The Sexual Harassment of Women at Workplace (Prevention, Prohibition and Redressal) Act, 2013, commonly called the POSH Act, provides a legal framework for preventing and addressing sexual harassment at the workplace.\n\nSexual harassment can include:\n\n- Unwelcome physical contact or advances\n- A demand or request for sexual favours\n- Sexually coloured remarks\n- Showing pornography\n- Other unwelcome physical, verbal or non-verbal conduct of a sexual nature\n\nHow to make a complaint:\n\n1. A written complaint can generally be made to the Internal Committee (IC) at the workplace.\n2. Where applicable, a complaint may be made to the Local Committee.\n3. A complaint should ordinarily be made within 3 months of the incident.\n4. The complaint period may be extended by the committee in appropriate circumstances, subject to the Act.\n5. The inquiry is to be completed within 90 days.\n6. The inquiry report is to be provided within 10 days after completion of the inquiry.\n\nDuring an inquiry, interim relief may be recommended in appropriate cases.\n\nProceedings under the Act are subject to confidentiality requirements.\n\nImportant: A complaint that cannot be proved is not automatically a false complaint. The Act separately deals with malicious or knowingly false complaints.','2026-09-03','Legal Aid Team','2026-09-03 12:06:16'),(4,'Women\'s Property Rights: What You Need to Know','Family Rights','Learn about women\'s property and inheritance rights, including important protections under the Hindu Succession Act.','Property rights depend on the type of property, the applicable personal law and the facts of the family situation. The information below focuses mainly on rights under Hindu succession law.\n\nDaughters and coparcenary property:\n\nUnder the Hindu Succession (Amendment) Act, 2005, a daughter of a coparcener can have the same coparcenary rights and liabilities as a son from birth, subject to the law and the facts of the case.\n\nSelf-acquired property:\n\nA person who legally owns self-acquired property generally has rights to deal with that property, subject to applicable law.\n\nInheritance:\n\nRights in parental, ancestral or other property can depend on whether the property is joint family property, self-acquired property, the existence of a will, and the applicable succession law.\n\nStreedhan:\n\nProperty or gifts belonging to a woman may have separate legal protection and should not simply be treated as the property of the husband or in-laws.\n\nWhat to keep:\n\n- Sale deeds and registration documents\n- Property tax records\n- Mutation or revenue records\n- Will, succession or settlement documents\n- Records of payments and ownership\n\nBecause property disputes can be fact-specific, legal advice should be obtained before taking major legal action. Free legal assistance may be available through NALSA and State Legal Services Authorities.','2026-09-03','Legal Aid Team','2026-09-03 12:06:16'),(5,'Cyber Safety for Women: Protecting Yourself Online','Cyber Safety','Practical steps for protecting yourself online and preserving evidence when facing cyber harassment or digital abuse.','Online harassment, cyberstalking, impersonation, privacy violations and other forms of digital abuse can cause serious harm.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- Repeated unwanted online contact\n- Cyberstalking\n- Sharing private images without consent\n- Fake or impersonating profiles\n- Threats and abusive messages\n- Online fraud or financial scams\n\nCurrent legal framework:\n\nCyber offences may be covered by different laws depending on the conduct. For example, Section 78 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 deals with stalking, including certain monitoring of a woman\'s internet, email or other electronic communication.\n\nThe Information Technology Act, 2000 also contains provisions dealing with certain privacy violations and sexually explicit material.\n\nHow to protect yourself:\n\n1. Use strong and unique passwords.\n2. Enable two-factor authentication.\n3. Review privacy and account-security settings.\n4. Do not share sensitive information or private images unnecessarily.\n5. Block or report abusive accounts where appropriate.\n6. Preserve screenshots, URLs, messages and original files before deleting anything.\n7. Note the date and time of important incidents.\n\nHow to report:\n\n- Use the National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal.\n- For cyber financial fraud, contact 1930 immediately.\n- Contact the local police or cyber crime unit when appropriate.\n- Call 112 when there is an immediate emergency.\n\nDo not alter or repeatedly edit original digital evidence. Preserve the original material safely where possible.','2026-09-03','Cyber Safety Team','2026-09-03 12:06:16'),(6,'Maternity Benefits: Your Rights as a Working Mother','Workplace Rights','Understand the main maternity protections available under the Maternity Benefit Act, 1961, as amended in 2017.','The Maternity Benefit Act, 1961, as amended in 2017, provides important protections for eligible women in covered establishments.\n\nKey benefits include:\n\n- Up to 26 weeks of maternity benefit for eligible women for the first two surviving children.\n- Up to 12 weeks for women with two or more surviving children, subject to the Act.\n- Certain adoptive and commissioning mothers are entitled to maternity benefit under the Act, subject to the statutory conditions.\n- Nursing breaks are provided after returning to work.\n- Establishments with 50 or more employees are required to provide a crèche facility, subject to the Act.\n- Where the nature of work permits, work from home may be allowed after maternity benefit for a mutually agreed period and conditions.\n\nEligibility and coverage depend on the Act and the type of establishment and employment.\n\nGovernment employees may be governed by separate service rules.\n\nIf an eligible maternity benefit is denied, a woman can seek help from the appropriate labour authority or obtain legal assistance.','2026-09-03','Legal Aid Team','2026-09-03 12:06:16'),(7,'Recognizing and Preventing Domestic Violence','Awareness & Prevention','Recognize warning signs of abuse, make a safety plan and support someone experiencing domestic violence.','Domestic violence can take different forms and may not always involve visible physical injuries.\n\nPossible forms of abuse include:\n\n- Physical abuse such as hitting, pushing or restraining\n- Emotional or verbal abuse such as humiliation, threats or intimidation\n- Economic abuse such as controlling money or access to financial resources\n- Sexual abuse\n- Controlling or threatening behaviour involving technology\n\nWarning signs may include:\n\n- Extreme jealousy or possessiveness\n- Isolation from friends and family\n- Repeated threats\n- Controlling finances or movement\n- Monitoring phones or online activity\n- Blaming the victim for the abuse\n- Destroying property\n\nSafety planning:\n\n1. Keep important documents somewhere safe.\n2. Save emergency contacts.\n3. Identify a safe place to go if necessary.\n4. Keep essential medicines and important items accessible.\n5. Preserve evidence where it is safe to do so.\n6. Know how to contact 112 and Women Helpline 181.\n\nHow to support someone:\n\n- Listen without judgment.\n- Believe and support them.\n- Avoid blaming them.\n- Respect their decisions and safety concerns.\n- Help them connect with appropriate professional support.\n\nAbuse is not the victim\'s fault. Help is available.','2026-09-03','Awareness Team','2026-09-03 12:06:16'),(8,'Dowry Law in India: Protection and Legal Recourse','Legal Rights','Understand the Dowry Prohibition Act and current criminal-law provisions concerning cruelty and dowry-related offences.','The Dowry Prohibition Act, 1961 prohibits the giving, taking and demanding of dowry, subject to the definitions and exceptions provided by law.\n\nDowry-related problems can also involve criminal offences under the current Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023.\n\nFor example:\n\n- Section 80 deals with dowry death.\n- Sections 85 and 86 deal with cruelty by a husband or relatives of a husband and define the relevant conduct.\n\nWhat a woman can do:\n\n1. Contact the police when a criminal offence is involved.\n2. Seek protection and other remedies under the Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005 where applicable.\n3. Approach a Protection Officer or One Stop Centre for support.\n4. Seek legal assistance through Legal Services Authorities.\n\nUseful records may include:\n\n- Messages or communications containing dowry demands\n- Bank or payment records\n- Records of gifts or Streedhan\n- Medical records where relevant\n- Witness details\n- Police complaint or FIR documents, if filed\n\nDifferent laws can provide different remedies, and the appropriate legal steps depend on the facts of the case. A woman can seek legal advice to understand which remedies apply to her situation.','2026-09-03','Legal Aid Team','2026-09-03 12:06:16'),(9,'student','safety','nothing to say','no',NULL,'FARHEEN KHAN','2026-09-12 18:43:29');
