INSERT INTO `women_legal_rights` VALUES (1,'domestic','Domestic Violence & Protection','bi-house-heart-fill','#db2777','Protection from physical, sexual, verbal, emotional and economic abuse in a domestic relationship.','Right to Protection from Domestic Violence','A woman facing domestic violence can seek legal protection against physical, sexual, verbal, emotional or economic abuse in a domestic relationship.','Protection covers physical, sexual, verbal and emotional abuse|Economic abuse is also covered|Threats and controlling behaviour can also amount to domestic violence|A Magistrate can issue a protection order to prevent further abuse','Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005 — Sections 3 and 18','Contact a Protection Officer, police authority, legal-aid service or Magistrate and seek appropriate protection and other relief.','2026-09-02 07:08:16'),(2,'domestic','Domestic Violence & Protection','bi-house-heart-fill','#db2777','Protection from physical, sexual, verbal, emotional and economic abuse in a domestic relationship.','Right to Residence in a Shared Household','A woman in a domestic relationship has the right to reside in the shared household, even if she does not own the property.','The right can exist even without ownership or title|She cannot be evicted or excluded except according to law|A Magistrate can grant a residence order|The court can also direct alternative accommodation or payment of rent in appropriate cases','Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005 — Sections 17 and 19','Approach the Magistrate through a Protection Officer, lawyer or legal-aid service and seek a residence order if you are being dispossessed or excluded.','2026-09-02 07:08:16'),(3,'domestic','Domestic Violence & Protection','bi-house-heart-fill','#db2777','Protection from physical, sexual, verbal, emotional and economic abuse in a domestic relationship.','Right to Monetary Relief','A woman affected by domestic violence can seek monetary relief for losses and expenses resulting from the violence.','Loss of earnings may be considered|Medical expenses may be covered|Damage or loss caused by domestic violence may be compensated|Monetary relief can also address maintenance for the woman and her children where appropriate','Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005 — Section 20','Apply to the Magistrate for monetary relief and provide available evidence of expenses, losses and financial impact.','2026-09-02 07:08:16'),(4,'domestic','Domestic Violence & Protection','bi-house-heart-fill','#db2777','Protection from physical, sexual, verbal, emotional and economic abuse in a domestic relationship.','Right to Temporary Custody of Children','In a domestic-violence proceeding, a Magistrate can grant temporary custody of a child to the aggrieved woman when appropriate.','Temporary custody can be ordered while proceedings are pending|The court can make arrangements for visitation|Visitation may be restricted where the court considers it necessary|The welfare and safety of the child are important considerations','Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005 — Section 21','Ask the Magistrate for a temporary custody order and explain any safety or welfare concerns affecting the child.','2026-09-02 07:08:16'),(5,'workplace','Workplace & Employment Rights','bi-briefcase-fill','#4f46e5','Equal pay, maternity benefits, safe working conditions and protection from gender-based discrimination and sexual harassment at work.','Right to Equal Pay and Protection from Gender Discrimination','Women and men must not be discriminated against in wages for the same work or work of a similar nature, and employers must not discriminate on the ground of sex while recruiting for such work, subject to lawful exceptions.','No gender discrimination in wages for the same or similar work|Same or similar work is assessed based on skill, effort, experience and responsibility|Employers cannot reduce another employee\'s wages simply to comply with equal-wage requirements|Sex-based discrimination in recruitment for the same or similar work is prohibited, subject to legal exceptions','Code on Wages, 2019 — Section 3','Keep employment records such as appointment letters, payslips and relevant communications, and approach the appropriate labour authority if you believe there is unlawful gender discrimination.','2026-09-02 07:13:54'),(6,'workplace','Workplace & Employment Rights','bi-briefcase-fill','#4f46e5','Equal pay, maternity benefits, safe working conditions and protection from gender-based discrimination and sexual harassment at work.','Right to Maternity Benefit','Eligible women employees are entitled to maternity benefits and related protections under the current social-security framework.','Eligibility generally requires at least 80 days of work in the relevant 12-month period|Maternity benefit can extend up to 26 weeks for eligible women|Up to 8 weeks may be taken before the expected delivery date|Eligible adopting and commissioning mothers can receive 12 weeks of maternity benefit in the circumstances specified by law|Nursing breaks and other maternity-related protections are provided by law','Code on Social Security, 2020 — Section 60 and related maternity-benefit provisions','Submit the required maternity-benefit notice and supporting documents to the employer and seek assistance from the appropriate labour authority or legal-aid service if the benefit is denied.','2026-09-02 07:13:54'),(7,'workplace','Workplace & Employment Rights','bi-briefcase-fill','#4f46e5','Equal pay, maternity benefits, safe working conditions and protection from gender-based discrimination and sexual harassment at work.','Right to a Workplace Free from Sexual Harassment','A woman has the right to a workplace free from sexual harassment and to a formal mechanism for making and resolving a complaint.','Sexual harassment includes unwelcome physical, verbal or non-verbal conduct of a sexual nature|The employer must provide a safe working environment|An Internal Committee is constituted in workplaces covered by the law|A Local Committee handles complaints in specified cases|A complaint is ordinarily made in writing within three months of the incident, subject to the extension provided by law|The inquiry should be completed within 90 days','Sexual Harassment of Women at Workplace Act, 2013 — Sections 3, 4, 6, 9 and 11','Submit a written complaint to the Internal Committee or, where applicable, the Local Committee. Keep copies of the complaint and relevant evidence.','2026-09-02 07:13:54'),(8,'workplace','Workplace & Employment Rights','bi-briefcase-fill','#4f46e5','Equal pay, maternity benefits, safe working conditions and protection from gender-based discrimination and sexual harassment at work.','Right Against Gender Discrimination in Recruitment','A woman should not be discriminated against on the ground of sex while being recruited for the same work or work of a similar nature, subject to lawful exceptions.','Sex-based discrimination in recruitment for the same or similar work is prohibited|The rule also covers conditions of employment|The law recognises limited exceptions where employment of women is prohibited or restricted by another law|For public employment, equality of opportunity is also protected by Article 16 of the Constitution','Code on Wages, 2019 — Section 3; Constitution of India — Article 16 for public employment','Keep the job advertisement, application records and communications showing the alleged discrimination, and approach the appropriate authority where necessary.','2026-09-02 07:13:54'),(9,'marriage','Marriage & Family Rights','bi-heart-fill','#db2777','Legal rights related to marriage, maintenance, divorce and protection against forced or unlawful marital practices.','Right to Choose a Spouse and Marry Freely','An adult woman has the right to choose whether and whom to marry, subject to the conditions prescribed by law.','An adult has the freedom to choose a spouse|Marriage must comply with the applicable personal or secular marriage law|Forced marriage violates the person’s freedom and may attract legal consequences|Family members cannot lawfully compel an adult woman to marry against her will','Constitution of India — Articles 14, 19 and 21; applicable marriage laws','If you are being forced into a marriage or threatened because of your choice of spouse, approach the police, a legal-aid service or the appropriate court for protection.','2026-09-02 07:15:38'),(10,'marriage','Marriage & Family Rights','bi-heart-fill','#db2777','Legal rights related to marriage, maintenance, divorce and protection against forced or unlawful marital practices.','Right to Maintenance','A woman who meets the legal conditions can seek financial maintenance from her husband or other person legally responsible for maintenance.','Maintenance may be available to a wife who is unable to maintain herself when her husband neglects or refuses to maintain her|The amount depends on the circumstances and may be ordered as a monthly allowance|Children may also have independent maintenance rights|The applicable personal or matrimonial law may provide additional remedies','Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 — Section 144','A woman seeking maintenance can approach the appropriate Magistrate or legal-aid service and provide information about income, expenses and family circumstances.','2026-09-02 07:15:38'),(11,'marriage','Marriage & Family Rights','bi-heart-fill','#db2777','Legal rights related to marriage, maintenance, divorce and protection against forced or unlawful marital practices.','Right to Seek Divorce or Other Matrimonial Relief','A woman can seek divorce or other matrimonial relief when the applicable marriage law provides a legal ground for it.','Divorce grounds depend on the marriage law applicable to the couple|Possible grounds can include cruelty, desertion and other legally recognised circumstances|The court decides the case according to the applicable law and evidence|Legal-aid services can help a woman understand available remedies','Hindu Marriage Act, 1955 — Section 13; other applicable personal or secular marriage laws','Consult a lawyer or legal-aid service to identify the marriage law and legal ground applicable to your situation and to understand the required court process.','2026-09-02 07:15:38'),(12,'marriage','Marriage & Family Rights','bi-heart-fill','#db2777','Legal rights related to marriage, maintenance, divorce and protection against forced or unlawful marital practices.','Right to Protection from Child Marriage','A girl below the legally prescribed minimum age of marriage is protected by law against child marriage.','The minimum legal age for marriage is 18 for women and 21 for men under the Prohibition of Child Marriage Act|Child marriage can attract penalties under the law|A child marriage may be voidable in circumstances provided by law|Courts can grant protection and related relief in appropriate cases','Prohibition of Child Marriage Act, 2006 — Sections 3, 9, 10 and 11','Report a child marriage or suspected child marriage to the police, Child Marriage Prohibition Officer or other appropriate authority and seek immediate protection where necessary.','2026-09-02 07:15:38'),(13,'property','Property & Inheritance Rights','bi-house-heart-fill','#7c3aed','Rights of women relating to ownership, inheritance and control of property under applicable Indian laws, including specific protections under Hindu succession law.','Right of a Daughter to Equal Coparcenary Rights','Under the Hindu Succession Act, a daughter of a coparcener has the same rights and liabilities in coparcenary property as a son.','A daughter becomes a coparcener by birth in a Mitakshara joint Hindu family|She has the same rights in coparcenary property as a son|She is subject to the same liabilities relating to the coparcenary property|The law protects qualifying transactions or partitions completed before 20 December 2004','Hindu Succession Act, 1956 — Section 6, as amended in 2005','If you believe your coparcenary share has been denied, collect property and family records and consult a lawyer or legal-aid service about your legal share.','2026-09-02 07:26:55'),(14,'property','Property & Inheritance Rights','bi-house-heart-fill','#7c3aed','Rights of women relating to ownership, inheritance and control of property under applicable Indian laws, including specific protections under Hindu succession law.','Right to Full Ownership of Property Held by a Hindu Woman','Property possessed by a female Hindu is generally held by her as a full owner rather than as a limited owner, subject to the exceptions provided by law.','The rule can apply to movable and immovable property|It can include property acquired by inheritance, gift, partition, purchase or other lawful means|Stridhana is included within the statutory framework|Certain instruments or court orders creating a restricted estate are treated separately under the law','Hindu Succession Act, 1956 — Section 14','Keep documents proving ownership or possession such as sale deeds, gift deeds, inheritance records and property records, and seek legal assistance if your ownership is unlawfully challenged.','2026-09-02 07:26:55'),(15,'property','Property & Inheritance Rights','bi-house-heart-fill','#7c3aed','Rights of women relating to ownership, inheritance and control of property under applicable Indian laws, including specific protections under Hindu succession law.','Right of a Daughter to Inherit as a Class I Heir','Under the Hindu Succession Act, a daughter is included among the Class I heirs and can inherit from her father in accordance with the rules of succession.','Daughters are listed as Class I heirs|Class I heirs succeed before heirs in later classes|The actual share depends on the family members surviving and the applicable succession rules|A will or other legally valid arrangement can affect succession in accordance with law','Hindu Succession Act, 1956 — Sections 8, 9 and 10; Schedule Class I','Obtain the relevant death, family and property records and consult the appropriate authority or legal-aid service to determine the applicable succession and share.','2026-09-02 07:26:55'),(16,'property','Property & Inheritance Rights','bi-house-heart-fill','#7c3aed','Rights of women relating to ownership, inheritance and control of property under applicable Indian laws, including specific protections under Hindu succession law.','Right of a Widow to a Share in Intestate Succession','Under the Hindu Succession Act, a widow is a Class I heir when her husband dies without a valid will, and she receives a share according to the statutory rules of succession.','A surviving widow is a Class I heir|Where there is more than one widow, they together take one share under the statutory rule|Other Class I heirs such as sons and daughters may also inherit|The final distribution depends on the surviving heirs and the applicable succession rules','Hindu Succession Act, 1956 — Sections 8, 9 and 10; Schedule Class I','Collect the deceased person’s death certificate, family details and property records, and obtain legal advice on the widow’s statutory share and succession process.','2026-09-02 07:26:55'),(17,'cyber','Cyber Safety & Online Harassment','bi-shield-lock-fill','#0891b2','Legal protections for women against online harassment, stalking, privacy violations, identity misuse and unlawful sharing of intimate or sexually explicit content.','Right Against Cyberstalking','A woman has legal protection against repeated unwanted following, monitoring or contacting, including through electronic communication, when the conduct falls within the offence of stalking.','Repeated unwanted online contact can amount to stalking when the legal conditions are satisfied|Monitoring a woman\'s use of the internet, email or other electronic communication can be covered|The law provides specified exceptions in certain circumstances|The offence can carry imprisonment and fine','Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 — Section 78','Preserve messages, call records, profile links, screenshots and other evidence, and report the matter to the police or through the National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal.','2026-09-02 07:28:09'),(18,'cyber','Cyber Safety & Online Harassment','bi-shield-lock-fill','#0891b2','Legal protections for women against online harassment, stalking, privacy violations, identity misuse and unlawful sharing of intimate or sexually explicit content.','Right to Protection of Privacy in Intimate Situations','A woman is protected against unlawful watching, capturing or dissemination of images of her while she is engaged in a private act where she has a reasonable expectation of privacy.','Watching a woman during a private act can be an offence|Capturing such images can be an offence|Disseminating such images can also be an offence|The law provides different punishment levels for first and subsequent convictions','Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 — Section 77 (Voyeurism)','Do not delete the original evidence if possible. Save screenshots, URLs and copies of the material and report the incident to the police or National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal.','2026-09-02 07:28:09'),(19,'cyber','Cyber Safety & Online Harassment','bi-shield-lock-fill','#0891b2','Legal protections for women against online harassment, stalking, privacy violations, identity misuse and unlawful sharing of intimate or sexually explicit content.','Right Against Identity Theft and Online Impersonation','A person whose password, electronic signature or other unique identification feature is used fraudulently or dishonestly without permission may be protected under the Information Technology Act.','Using another person\'s password or unique identification information without permission can be an offence|Fraudulent or dishonest use of another person\'s identity information can attract criminal liability|Online impersonation may involve additional offences depending on the conduct|Keep evidence of fake profiles, messages and transactions','Information Technology Act, 2000 — Sections 66C and 66D','Preserve screenshots, profile URLs, emails and transaction records, report the account to the platform and submit a cybercrime complaint through the National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal.','2026-09-02 07:28:09'),(20,'cyber','Cyber Safety & Online Harassment','bi-shield-lock-fill','#0891b2','Legal protections for women against online harassment, stalking, privacy violations, identity misuse and unlawful sharing of intimate or sexually explicit content.','Right to Protection Against Unlawful Sharing of Sexually Explicit Material Online','Publishing or transmitting sexually explicit material in electronic form can attract criminal liability under the Information Technology Act.','Electronic publication or transmission of sexually explicit material can be an offence|The law provides punishment for such conduct|Different provisions apply to material involving children|Preserve evidence before requesting removal where possible','Information Technology Act, 2000 — Section 67A; Section 67B for material involving children','Save the relevant evidence and report the content through the National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal and the concerned platform. In an emergency, contact the police.','2026-09-02 07:28:09'),(21,'sexual','Sexual Harassment Rights','bi-shield-fill-check','#e11d48','Legal protections against sexual harassment, unwanted sexual conduct, sexual intimidation and other offences affecting the dignity and safety of women.','Right Against Sexual Harassment','A woman is legally protected against specified forms of sexual harassment, including unwelcome sexual advances, demands for sexual favours, showing pornography against her will and sexually coloured remarks.','Unwelcome physical contact or sexual advances can constitute sexual harassment|Demanding or requesting sexual favours is covered|Showing pornography against a woman\'s will is covered|Sexually coloured remarks are also covered|Punishment depends on the specific act involved','Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 — Section 75','Preserve relevant messages, recordings or other evidence where lawfully available and report the incident to the police or seek legal assistance.','2026-09-02 07:29:12'),(22,'sexual','Sexual Harassment Rights','bi-shield-fill-check','#e11d48','Legal protections against sexual harassment, unwanted sexual conduct, sexual intimidation and other offences affecting the dignity and safety of women.','Right to Protection from Sexual Assault or Criminal Force','A woman is protected against assault or use of criminal force when it is intended, or known to be likely, to outrage her modesty.','Unwanted physical force may constitute an offence|The intention or knowledge required by law is relevant|The offence can carry imprisonment and fine|The circumstances and evidence of each case matter','Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 — Section 74','Move to a safe place and report the incident to the police. Preserve relevant evidence and seek medical or legal assistance where necessary.','2026-09-02 07:29:12'),(23,'sexual','Sexual Harassment Rights','bi-shield-fill-check','#e11d48','Legal protections against sexual harassment, unwanted sexual conduct, sexual intimidation and other offences affecting the dignity and safety of women.','Right to Protection from Forced Disrobing','A woman is protected against assault or criminal force used with the intention of disrobing her or compelling her to be naked.','Using criminal force with the intention of disrobing is an offence|Attempting to compel a woman to be naked is covered|The offence can attract imprisonment and fine|Physical, digital and other related conduct may involve additional offences depending on the facts','Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 — Section 76','Report the incident to the police and preserve relevant physical or digital evidence. Seek immediate assistance where there is an ongoing threat.','2026-09-02 07:29:12'),(24,'sexual','Sexual Harassment Rights','bi-shield-fill-check','#e11d48','Legal protections against sexual harassment, unwanted sexual conduct, sexual intimidation and other offences affecting the dignity and safety of women.','Right to Protection Against Acts or Conduct Insulting a Woman\'s Modesty or Privacy','A woman is protected against words, sounds, gestures, objects or intrusion on privacy when the conduct is intended to insult her modesty or invades her privacy.','Words or gestures can fall within the offence when the required intention is present|Sounds or acts directed at a woman can also be covered|Intrusion upon a woman\'s privacy is expressly included|The circumstances and intention of the accused are relevant','Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 — Section 79','Preserve messages, recordings, screenshots or other relevant evidence and report the matter to the police or seek assistance from a legal-aid service.','2026-09-02 07:29:12'),(25,'police','Police & Legal Proceedings','bi-bank2','#2563eb','Important procedural rights for women when reporting offences, giving statements and seeking legal assistance during criminal proceedings.','Right to Report a Cognizable Offence to the Police','Information relating to a cognizable offence can be given to the officer in charge of a police station, including through electronic communication in accordance with the law.','Information about a cognizable offence may be given irrespective of the area where the offence was committed|Information given electronically is recognised subject to the procedure prescribed by law|The information must be recorded according to the law|A copy of the recorded information is to be given to the informant without charge','Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 — Section 173','Provide the facts and available evidence to the police and keep a copy of the complaint or acknowledgement. If necessary, seek assistance from a legal-aid service.','2026-09-02 07:33:01'),(26,'police','Police & Legal Proceedings','bi-bank2','#2563eb','Important procedural rights for women when reporting offences, giving statements and seeking legal assistance during criminal proceedings.','Right to Have a Rape Victim\'s Statement Recorded at a Place of Her Choice','In a rape case, the statement of the victim is to be recorded at her residence or at a place of her choice and, as far as practicable, by a woman police officer in the manner prescribed by law.','The statement may be recorded at the victim\'s residence or another place of her choice|As far as practicable, a woman police officer should record the statement|The law provides for the presence of specified supportive persons in the circumstances stated in the provision|Audio-video electronic means may also be used for recording the statement','Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 — Section 176(1)','Tell the investigating officer that you want the statement recorded at your residence or another place of your choice, and seek legal assistance where necessary.','2026-09-02 07:33:01'),(27,'police','Police & Legal Proceedings','bi-bank2','#2563eb','Important procedural rights for women when reporting offences, giving statements and seeking legal assistance during criminal proceedings.','Right to Free Legal Aid','A woman is entitled to seek free legal services through the legal services authorities, irrespective of her income or financial status.','Women are specifically included among persons entitled to free legal services|Legal aid can include legal advice and representation, subject to the applicable legal-services process|Applications can be made through the State, District or Taluk legal services authorities|NALSA provides a national legal-aid helpline at 15100','Legal Services Authorities Act, 1987 — Section 12(c)','Contact the nearest Legal Services Authority or call NALSA\'s toll-free legal-aid helpline at 15100 to seek legal assistance.','2026-09-02 07:33:01'),(28,'police','Police & Legal Proceedings','bi-bank2','#2563eb','Important procedural rights for women when reporting offences, giving statements and seeking legal assistance during criminal proceedings.','Right to Know the Progress of a Criminal Investigation','The law provides for the informant or victim to be informed about the progress or action taken in the investigation in the manner prescribed by law.','The investigating process is subject to the procedure laid down in BNSS|The police report on completion of investigation is submitted to the Magistrate as required by law|The law provides for communication of investigation progress in specified circumstances|Keep the complaint number and contact details so that you can follow up appropriately','Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 — Section 193 and related investigation provisions','Keep your complaint or case reference number and contact the investigating officer or appropriate police authority for updates. Seek legal aid when necessary.','2026-09-02 07:33:01'),(29,'general','General Women\'s Rights','bi-person-vcard-fill','#be185d','Important constitutional and legal protections supporting equality, dignity, personal liberty and protection of women from discrimination and violence.','Right to Equality Before the Law','Women are entitled to equality before the law and equal protection of the laws, and the State cannot discriminate against citizens solely on the ground of sex.','Every person is entitled to equality before the law|Sex-based discrimination by the State is prohibited subject to constitutionally permitted provisions|The Constitution allows special provisions for women and children|Government schemes and protective measures can therefore lawfully provide special support to women','Constitution of India — Articles 14 and 15','If you believe a government action or public authority has discriminated against you unlawfully, seek advice from a legal-aid service or the appropriate authority.','2026-09-02 07:36:44'),(30,'general','General Women\'s Rights','bi-person-vcard-fill','#be185d','Important constitutional and legal protections supporting equality, dignity, personal liberty and protection of women from discrimination and violence.','Right to Life, Personal Liberty and Dignity','Every woman is protected by the constitutional right to life and personal liberty. The protection of personal dignity is an important part of this fundamental right.','No person can be deprived of life or personal liberty except according to procedure established by law|The right protects personal freedom and dignity|State authorities must act according to law when restricting liberty|Other laws provide additional protections for specific forms of violence or discrimination','Constitution of India — Article 21','If your life, liberty or dignity is threatened, seek immediate protection from the police or appropriate authority and obtain legal assistance where necessary.','2026-09-02 07:36:44'),(31,'general','General Women\'s Rights','bi-person-vcard-fill','#be185d','Important constitutional and legal protections supporting equality, dignity, personal liberty and protection of women from discrimination and violence.','Right to Constitutional Remedies','A person whose fundamental rights are violated can approach the appropriate constitutional court for enforcement of those rights.','Article 32 provides a remedy before the Supreme Court for enforcement of fundamental rights|High Courts can also exercise writ jurisdiction under Article 226|Courts can issue appropriate writs in cases within their jurisdiction|Legal-aid services may assist eligible persons in pursuing appropriate remedies','Constitution of India — Articles 32 and 226','Where a fundamental right is seriously violated, seek advice from a lawyer or legal-aid service about approaching the appropriate High Court or Supreme Court.','2026-09-02 07:36:44'),(32,'general','General Women\'s Rights','bi-person-vcard-fill','#be185d','Important constitutional and legal protections supporting equality, dignity, personal liberty and protection of women from discrimination and violence.','Right to Protection Against Cruelty by Husband or His Relatives','A woman is protected against specified forms of cruelty by her husband or his relatives, including conduct likely to cause grave harm or harassment connected with unlawful demands for property or valuable security.','Cruelty includes certain wilful conduct likely to drive a woman to suicide or cause grave injury or danger to her life, limb or health|Harassment connected with unlawful demands for property or valuable security is covered|The offence applies to a husband or relative of the husband|The provision is distinct from the civil protection and relief available under the Domestic Violence Act','Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 — Sections 85 and 86','If you face such cruelty, move to a safe place, preserve relevant evidence and contact the police or a legal-aid service for appropriate protection and legal action.','2026-09-02 07:36:44');
